<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Johannesburg</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Joburg, Egoli</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Johannesburg is the largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> and the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauteng">Gauteng</a> province. It was founded in 1886 after the discovery of gold on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/witwatersrand">Witwatersrand</a> and grew within a decade into a mining city of over 100,000 people, many of them foreign uitlanders, whose grievances fed the conflict that led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-boer-war">Second Boer War</a>. It remains the country's financial and industrial centre.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/johannesburg.jpg" alt="Johannesburg" width="320" height="125" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mark Hillary · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Johannesburg_skyline_2017.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Johannesburg</b>, founded that year, had more than 100,000 inhabitants by 1896, and by the late 1890s the Rand produced roughly a quarter of the world’s gold.</blockquote><a href="/empire/south-african-republic">South African Republic · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Nelson Mandela (1918–2013), a lawyer who opened a practice with Oliver Tambo in <b>Johannesburg</b> in 1952, was “volunteer-in-chief” of the Defiance Campaign and the first commander of Umkhonto we Sizwe, and in twenty-seven years in prison, eighteen of them on Robben Island, he became the world’s best-known political…</blockquote><a href="/movement/anti-apartheid-movement">Anti-apartheid movement · People</a></li><li><blockquote>In <b>Johannesburg</b> he worked as a packer at Eric Gallo's record-pressing plant, the only one in black Africa, and the firm's talent scout brought the choir into the studio, where companies sought Zulu close-harmony singing to sell to migrant mineworkers.</blockquote><a href="/songs/mbube">Mbube</a></li><li><blockquote>&quot;Nkosi Sikelel' iAfrika&quot; (&quot;Lord Bless Africa&quot;) was composed in 1897 by Enoch Sontonga, a Xhosa teacher at a Methodist mission school near <b>Johannesburg</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/nkosi-sikelel-iafrika">Nkosi Sikelel' iAfrika</a></li><li><blockquote>It was also the name of a dance popular in the shebeens of <b>Johannesburg</b>'s townships in the mid-1950s, in which partners patted each other in time to the music.</blockquote><a href="/songs/pata-pata">Pata Pata</a></li><li><blockquote>Born in rural Natal, Linda was a migrant worker and beer-hall singer who led an a cappella group, the Evening Birds, and worked as a packer at Eric Gallo's record-pressing plant in <b>Johannesburg</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/the-lion-sleeps-tonight">The Lion Sleeps Tonight</a></li></ul></section>
